# The abstraction: three client incarnations of one principal in the exhaustive
# search (four windows over two principals in conformance), sequence numbers 1
# and 2 per window, payload digests 0 and 1, outcomes Pending / Accepted(slot) /
# Rejected, a retained floor 0..=2, a two-entry capacity (three in conformance)
# and a per-principal incarnation limit and scope budget. `admit` is a probe
# (&self); `record` requires the model's Fresh answer; `settle` requires Pending;
# `acknowledge` raises the floor only when the scope already has a window;
# `retire` removes a window wholesale (the one forgetting transition without a
# floor, which is why the client rule forbids retrying a retired incarnation).
#
# The six claims checked on every reachable state and every transition:
#   R1 a repeated matching request is answered with its recorded result, never
#      Fresh, and a recorded id is not recorded again;
#   R2 the same id with a different digest is a Conflict and changes nothing;
#   R3 a sequence at or below the floor is refused and never revived;
#   R4 a settled outcome is not rewritten;
#   R5 incarnations are separate namespaces and a step changes no other scope;
#   R6 retained entries, live windows and per-principal incarnations stay inside
#      their bounds, and a full book refuses rather than evicting.
#
# Differential conformance drives the real AdmissionBook with the same legal
# steps and compares the verdict, every window's digest/outcome/floor and the
# retained count after every step.

